{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 commits a circular-argument fallacy: the conclusion 'the adjudication-ritual is irreparably broken' is entailed by, and identical in force to, the unsupported premise '§11.3 cannot be patched.' The only offered support is an appeal to SRC_004, but no evidence shows SRC_004 actually forbids the mechanistic assumptions needed. The target thus assumes what it sets out to prove: if the impossibility premise is false, there is no brokenness; and if it is true, the target has merely restated it, not demonstrated it.","premise":"SRC_001's argument depends on a circular proof: it asserts that §11.3 cannot be patched, then uses that asserted impossibility to classify the ritual as irreparably broken. A critic whose conclusion is identical to its unsupported premise establishes nothing.","target":"25f804cfe5e27b5778d675d3534a8f922d2112899ffb1b7b4c4157ad25650867"}]}